window.docContents[141].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--July___-2025":{"contents":"The book has been updated for version 4.21 of Lean.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Introduction\u0009Release history","header":"July, 2025","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--July___-2025"},"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--September___-2022":{"contents":"This release adds the first half of a chapter on type classes, which are Lean's mechanism for overloading operators and an important means of organizing code and structuring libraries. Additionally, the second chapter has been updated to account for changes in Lean's stream API.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Introduction\u0009Release history","header":"September, 2022","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--September___-2022"},"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Programming___-Proving___-and-Performance--Proving-Equivalence--Exercise--More-Accumulator-Proofs--Factorial":{"contents":"Prove that  from the exercises in the previous section is equal to your tail-recursive solution by finding the relationship between the accumulator and the result and proving a suitable helper theorem.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009Proving Equivalence\u0009Exercise\u0009More Accumulator Proofs","header":"8.2.4.2.2. Factorial","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Programming___-Proving___-and-Performance--Proving-Equivalence--Exercise--More-Accumulator-Proofs--Factorial"},"/#alternative-recovery":{"contents":" can also be used in situations where there is more than one way for input to be acceptable.\nFor the input form , an alternative set of business rules that implement conventions from a legacy system might be the following:\n\n1. All human users must provide a birth year that is four digits.2. Users born prior to 1970 do not need to provide names, due to incomplete older records.3. Users born after 1970 must provide names.4. Companies should enter  as their year of birth and provide a company name.\n\nNo particular provision is made for users born in 1970.\nIt is expected that they will either give up, lie about their year of birth, or call.\nThe company considers this an acceptable cost of doing business.\n\nThe following inductive type captures the values that can be produced from these stated rules:\n\n\n\nA validator for these rules is more complicated, however, as it must address all three cases.\nWhile it can be written as a series of nested if expressions, it's easier to design the three cases independently and then combine them.\nThis requires a means of recovering from failure while preserving error messages:\n\n\n\nThis pattern of recovery from failures is common enough that Lean has built-in syntax for it, attached to a type class named :\n\n\n\nThe expression  is short for .\nAn instance of  for  allows this syntax to be used for error recovery:\n\n\n\nThe validator for  can be built from a validator for each constructor.\nThe rules for a company state that the birth year should be the string  and that the name should be non-empty.\nThe constructor , however, has no representation of the birth year at all, so there's no easy way to carry it out using .\nThe key is to use a function with  that ignores its argument.\n\nChecking that a Boolean condition holds without recording any evidence of this fact in a type can be accomplished with :\n\n\n\nThis definition of  uses , and then throws away the resulting  value:\n\n\n\nHowever, this definition is quite noisy.\nIt can be simplified in two ways.\nThe first is to replace the first use of  with a specialized version that automatically ignores the value returned by the first argument, called .\nThis operator is also controlled by a type class, called , and  is syntactic sugar for :\n\n\n\nThere is a default implementation of  in terms of : seqRight (a : f α) (b : Unit → f β) : f β := pure (fun _ x => x) <*> a <*> b ().\n\nUsing ,  becomes simpler:\n\n\n\nOne more simplification is possible.\nFor every ,  is equivalent to .\nIn other words, using  to apply a function that was placed into the  type using  is overkill, and the function could have just been applied using .\nThis simplification yields:\n\n\n\nThe remaining two constructors of  use subtypes for their fields.\nA general-purpose tool for checking subtypes will make these easier to read:\n\n\n\nIn the function's argument list, it's important that the type class  occur after the specification of the arguments  and .\nOtherwise, it would refer to an additional set of automatic implicit arguments, rather than to the manually-provided values.\nThe  instance is what allows the proposition  to be checked using if.\n\nThe two human cases do not need any additional tools:\n\n\n\n\n\nThe validators for the three cases can be combined using :\n\n\n\nThe successful cases return constructors of , as expected:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe worst possible input returns all the possible failures:\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Alternatives","header":"5.4.1. Recovery from Failure","id":"/#alternative-recovery"},"/#finishing-splitList-shorter-proof":{"contents":"Now that both helper theorems have been proved, the rest of  will be completed quickly.\nThe current proof state has one goal remaining:\n\n\n\nUsing  together with the right✝ assumption completes the proof:\n\n\n\nThe next step is to return to the actual theorem that is needed to prove that merge sort terminates: that so long as a list has at least two entries, both results of splitting it are strictly shorter.\n\n\n\n\n\nPattern matching works just as well in tactic scripts as it does in programs.\nBecause  has at least two entries, they can be exposed with match, which also refines the type through dependent pattern matching:\n\n\n\n\n\nSimplifying using  removes  and , resulting in the computed lengths of lists each gaining a :\n\n\n\n\n\nReplacing  with  removes these s, because  makes use of the fact that  implies :\n\n\n\n\n\nThis goal now matches , which can be used to conclude the proof:\n\n\n\nThe facts needed to prove that  terminates can be pulled out of the resulting :\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009More Inequalities\u0009Splitting a List Makes it Shorter","header":"8.4.2.2. Finishing the Proof","id":"/#finishing-splitList-shorter-proof"}});